  "summary": "KQED reports that as several Bay Area cities scale back or terminate Flock ALPR contracts, San Francisco officials \u2014 including Mayor Daniel Lurie and DA Brooke Jenkins \u2014 are defending the city's roughly 400-camera network ahead of a February 2027 contract renewal. The city shortened data retention from one year to 30 days after an audit found federal and out-of-state agencies improperly searched SFPD Flock data hundreds of times, but activists continue protesting and demand the cameras be removed.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "In June, SFPD announced that its Flock data had been inappropriately searched hundreds of times by federal and other out-of-state agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 14
    },
    {
      "quote": "Lurie announced last month that the city would restrict the number of days that San Francisco Police officers can access license plate reader data from one year to 30 days after it is captured.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "Richmond, Santa Cruz, El Cerrito and Los Altos Hills have all pulled back or canceled their Flock contracts over concerns about privacy and data-sharing, as have several cities in Santa Clara County.",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    },
    {
      "quote": "Berkeley voted to continue its relationship with Flock, but did not pass a proposed expansion of the technology. In Oakland, the City Council voted in December to renew and expand its Flock network.",
      "paragraph_idx": 31
    }
  ],
